WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.360
In our last video, we talked about&nbsp;
the importance and the difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.520
of protecting personally identifiable&nbsp;
information through anonymity in big data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.960
Now, anonymity means that individuals cannot be&nbsp;
identified. Another important element of privacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.880
however, is confidentiality for what’s called&nbsp;
nonpublic information. In its simplest form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.920
confidentiality means that regardless of whether&nbsp;
individuals can be identified within the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:43.840
their data will not be shared with people&nbsp;
they did not specifically allow to see it.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.600
The first is that in conducting transactions,&nbsp;
companies do share limited amounts of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.680
with third parties. For example, if a person&nbsp;
goes to make a large purchase, it’s not uncommon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.760
for the vendor to call the bank and ask whether&nbsp;
the person has enough money in the bank to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.920
that purchase. They’re just checking for what’s&nbsp;
called sufficient funds. And all the bank does is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.680
tell them yes or no. And so, there’s a sharing&nbsp;
of a very small amount of information, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.560
bank does not pass along the person’s account&nbsp;
numbers, their ID information, doesn’t give them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
their actual balance; just says whether they&nbsp;
have enough to cover a particular transaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.560
On the other hand, it is also sometimes the&nbsp;
case that information is stolen from companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.640
Several companies have had their data stolen,&nbsp;
including credit card information, addresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.160
and other important personal information. And&nbsp;
in some cases, companies have lost hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.800
millions of dollars in business because consumers&nbsp;
were no longer confident that their information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.560
would be safe.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.680
Similarly, another limitation to confidentiality&nbsp;
is that companies sometimes had to give their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.120
information to courts or government&nbsp;
regulators as part of law suits that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.800
could’ve put them out of business completely&nbsp;
if they did not provide the information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.640
It’s a very awkward situation, but it does&nbsp;
come up occasionally. The trick with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.680
one is while it is a legal process, it is not&nbsp;
something that the users originally agreed to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.320
and so there is a violation of trust even if&nbsp;
what’s happening is technically a legal process.&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.160
However, these exceptions don’t call for a&nbsp;
complete lockdown on data because the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.240
does provide very important services, but it does&nbsp;
call for more care and attention. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.280
the National Science Foundation and the National&nbsp;
Institutes of Health have instituted policies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.600
researchers have to present a plan to provide&nbsp;
access to their data they use in their studies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.800
Additionally, there are insurance companies&nbsp;
that now provide insurance for the cost of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.720
breaches and companies should consider whether&nbsp;
they want to purchase that kind of insurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.640
Finally, companies should very&nbsp;
carefully consider whether they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.600
even need to have the confidential&nbsp;
information in the first place.&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.120
It’s clear that, things can go very wrong when&nbsp;
data is supposed to be confidential. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.280
this doesn’t call for a complete avoidance&nbsp;
of data with non-public information in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.960
After all, that’s what makes things like&nbsp;
restaurant and movie recommendations work so well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.720
and it’s certainly important for using&nbsp;
big data to make medical discoveries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.400
Some of these benefits are of personal&nbsp;
conveniences and could theoretically be forgone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.000
but others have life and death consequences and&nbsp;
deserve to be maintained. For these reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.400
it’s important for companies to consider how&nbsp;
they’re going to deal with non-public information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.080
That way, the trust of regular people, who are&nbsp;
the consumers and people who benefit from big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:45.840
data research, the trust can be maintained,&nbsp;
and big data can provide its full benefits.

